\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The same state in two coordinates. Bottom: the ascent state $\bm  {u}$, the target $\bm  {x}_0$ plus noise $\sigma (t)$, which grows without bound toward $t=1$. Top: what the network is fed, the bridge sample \eqref  {eq:x}, whose noise $\sigma _{\mathrm  {sb}}(t)$ stays below $\tau $ and vanishes at both ends. The optimal outputs agree, $\mathbb  {E}[\bm  {x}_0\mid \bm  {x}_t,\bm  {x}_1] = \mathbb  {E}[\bm  {x}_0\mid \bm  {u},\bm  {x}_1]$. Radii drawn to scale for $V=1$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{1}{3}{The same state in two coordinates. Bottom: the ascent state $\uu $, the target $\x _0$ plus noise $\sigma (t)$, which grows without bound toward $t=1$. Top: what the network is fed, the bridge sample \eqref {eq:x}, whose noise $\ssb (t)$ stays below $\tau $ and vanishes at both ends. The optimal outputs agree, $\E [\x _0\mid \x _t,\x _1] = \E [\x _0\mid \uu ,\x _1]$. Radii drawn to scale for $V=1$}{figure.1}{}}
